1
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:04,060
Previously on Friends:

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:06,980
How did the job stuff go? He offered me one!

3
00:00:06,980 --> 00:00:09,920
You know what? This calls for a bottle of lsrael's finest!

4
00:00:12,840 --> 00:00:14,420
The job is in Paris.

5
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,800
Oh, God, please, somebody say something.

6
00:00:23,300 --> 00:00:26,980
So if you take this job, you'll be moving to Paris?

7
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:29,160
Or facing a bitch of a commute.

8
00:00:31,818 --> 00:00:37,779
I know, it's huge, and it's scary and it's really far away from you guys.

9
00:00:37,800 --> 00:00:39,940
But this is such an incredible opportunity for me.

10
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:42,540
I've already told them about our situation with Emma,

11
00:00:43,060 --> 00:00:46,280
and they said they'll do whatever we need to make us feel comfortable. OK.

12
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:50,860
I mean, I'll fly back and forth, they'll fly you out. Anything we want.

13
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:53,420
My boss said I might be getting a new lamp in my cubicle.

14
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,080
All right. We'll work it out. Oh, thank you. Thank you.

15
00:01:07,160 --> 00:01:10,120
You sure this is what you want? I think it is.

16
00:01:14,520 --> 00:01:18,860
What's going on? I got a really incredible job offer.

17
00:01:19,080 --> 00:01:20,280
Hey, great! All right! Good for you!

18
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,000
It's in Paris. What?

19
00:01:23,514 --> 00:01:29,419
No, no, no! No, no, no! No, too much is changing, okay?

20
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:32,220
First Phoebe getting married. Congratulations.

21
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:36,340
And then these two move in to a stupid house in the stupid suburbs.

22
00:01:36,460 --> 00:01:38,760
Hey, this afternoon you said you'd be supportive.

23
00:01:39,080 --> 00:01:40,880
It comes and goes. I wouldn't trust it!

24
00:01:43,240 --> 00:01:45,740
You guys, this is really, really important to me.

25
00:01:45,960 --> 00:01:47,900
It means a lot if you could try to get onboard.

26
00:01:48,160 --> 00:01:50,900
Of course we can. Congratulations. Thank you.

27
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:54,305
Joey?

28
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:57,120
No, my hugs are reserved for people staying in America!

29
00:01:58,740 --> 00:01:59,260
Joey, it would mean...

30
00:01:59,620 --> 00:02:02,040
No! Get your France-going arms away from me!

31
00:02:05,200 --> 00:02:08,220
Are you okay with this? Well, it makes me feel sad, but...

32
00:02:08,260 --> 00:02:09,400
Talking to Ross. I see.

33
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:16,380
Rachel moving to another country? Not being able to see her every day?

34
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:18,100
How could I be okay with this?

35
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:21,182
But what are we gonna do? She really needs this job.

36
00:02:21,202 --> 00:02:26,936
Do you think if Ralph Lauren offered her her old job back, she would take it?

37
00:02:27,186 --> 00:02:28,244
How's that gonna happen?

38
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:30,800
Is this the best way to use one of your three magic wishes?

39
00:02:32,300 --> 00:02:35,260
Like, I don't know, I could talk to her boss. Yeah.

40
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:38,740
I met him at that Christmas party. I mean, we really hit it off.

41
00:02:39,080 --> 00:02:42,260
The guy who kept calling you "Ron"? I didn't say we were brothers!

42
00:03:22,087 --> 00:03:23,148
Hey, Phoebe. Hey. Hi.

43
00:03:24,120 --> 00:03:29,000
Tell me what you think. The house next door to the one that we're buying just went on the market.

44
00:03:29,420 --> 00:03:31,140
I wanna take a look at it, but Chandler doesn't.

45
00:03:31,160 --> 00:03:32,760
We close escrow tomorrow.

46
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:36,660
So seeing another house can only confuse us. We're easily confused. We're not very bright.

47
00:03:38,860 --> 00:03:41,500
But what if it's better than ours? Shouldn't we at least look?

48
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:42,820
What do you think, Pheebs?

49
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:47,200
Well, I think that shirt makes you look like you should work at a Baskin-Robbins.

50
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:53,838
Anyway....

51
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:58,220
Hey, isn't Joey's agent Estelle Leonard? Yeah.

52
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:00,100
She died. You're kidding!

53
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:02,620
That's terrible. Yeah, last Saturday.

54
00:04:03,060 --> 00:04:07,080
Wow! She was the first black man to fly solo across the Atlantic!

55
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:12,280
Wait a minute, I read the wrong one. Oh, yeah?

56
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,260
Yeah, she was just an agent.

57
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:21,660
Joey's gonna be so upset. I know. He always wanted to be the
first black man to cross the Atlantic.

58
00:04:24,500 --> 00:04:26,040
We cannot tell Joey about this.

59
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:28,500
He's already flipping out about everything changing.

60
00:04:28,960 --> 00:04:30,460
This'll push him over the edge.

61
00:04:30,500 --> 00:04:33,260
Seriously, you don't think we should tell him? Not for a little while.

62
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:36,380
Let's just give him a few days to get used to everything else.

63
00:04:36,580 --> 00:04:37,880
What if he reads the paper?

64
00:04:38,260 --> 00:04:40,820
Unless Snoopy says it to Charlie Brown, I think we're okay.

65
00:04:48,380 --> 00:04:51,260
May I help you? Yeah. I'm a friend of Rachel Green's.

66
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:55,160
Actually, we met at the Christmas party about two years ago.

67
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:58,220
Oh, right. Don? Close. Ron.

68
00:05:01,980 --> 00:05:03,280
Well, what can I do for you?

69
00:05:03,800 --> 00:05:07,820
Well, I'm here to see if you'll give Rachel her job back.

70
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:11,160
Did she ask you to come here and do this?

71
00:05:12,060 --> 00:05:13,220
First I have to get you to agree...

72
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:14,800
...then we'll see if she wants to come back.

73
00:05:17,060 --> 00:05:18,440
Wow, that is tempting.

74
00:05:20,240 --> 00:05:26,920
She loved her job here. Let's face it, you're not gonna find anyone who did it as well as she did. Isn't that true?

75
00:05:27,620 --> 00:05:30,760
She is good. I took a shot there.

76
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:34,080
But I'm sorry, there's nothing I can do.

77
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:37,440
Oh, that's not true. There's nothing I want to do.

78
00:05:39,260 --> 00:05:40,040
I see.

79
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:42,560
Thanks very much.

80
00:05:48,160 --> 00:05:50,720
Is this your son? Yeah, his name is Ross.

81
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:59,540
What? Nothing, it's just that it's close to Ron.

82
00:06:04,060 --> 00:06:06,200
Does little Ross like dinosaurs, by any chance?

83
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:08,400
Yeah, they're all he talks about. Why?

84
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:18,040
How would he like to come with me to the Museum of Natural History after everyone else has left, just the two of us...

85
00:06:18,580 --> 00:06:20,940
...and he can touch anything he wants?

86
00:06:27,000 --> 00:06:30,000
I just heard it how you must have heard it, and that's not good.

87
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:33,080
Let me start again. I'm a paleontologist.

88
00:06:33,860 --> 00:06:38,060
You'll be there with us, and the touching refers only to bones-- fossils!

89
00:06:42,580 --> 00:06:46,140
You could really arrange that? You let Rachel come back and it's done.

90
00:06:48,270 --> 00:06:50,880
I guess having Rachel back wouldn't be the worst thing in the world.

91
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:54,280
Yes! Thank you! This is great! Thank you so much.

92
00:06:54,400 --> 00:06:56,540
I swear, your kid's gonna have the time of his life.

93
00:06:56,640 --> 00:06:58,520
That's great. I worry about little Ross.

94
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:02,420
He's always reading, collecting rocks and he's obsessed with dinosaurs.

95
00:07:02,440 --> 00:07:03,200
He'll be fine!

96
00:07:06,620 --> 00:07:08,100
Hey, Pheebs. Hey.

97
00:07:08,920 --> 00:07:11,380
Everything okay? I'm just mad at my agent.

98
00:07:11,727 --> 00:07:13,320
Estelle? Why?

99
00:07:13,340 --> 00:07:16,840
There's a part in this TV movie that I would be perfect for...

100
00:07:17,060 --> 00:07:19,920
...and I didn't even get put up for it. She better have a good reason.

101
00:07:22,280 --> 00:07:23,520
I'm guessing she does.

102
00:07:23,840 --> 00:07:27,520
Well, I wanna hear it, because she keeps doing this.

103
00:07:27,960 --> 00:07:30,880
No, wait, wait, wait! All right, I gotta go.

104
00:07:31,280 --> 00:07:34,860
Just listen. Promise me that you will wait a minute before you call her.

105
00:07:35,320 --> 00:07:36,440
Well, okay. Why?

106
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:42,840
Because a promise between friends means never having to give a reason.

107
00:07:47,480 --> 00:07:48,740
I love that saying!

108
00:07:58,200 --> 00:08:00,320
Hello? Joey, it's Estelle.

109
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:04,680
I was just gonna call you. That's weird.

110
00:08:05,180 --> 00:08:08,020
It's a little coincidental, but believable.

111
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:15,960
Listen, I'm sure you're wondering why I didn't get you an audition for that TV movie.

112
00:08:16,180 --> 00:08:17,340
Yeah, actually I am.

113
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:19,640
I guess I dropped the ball there.

114
00:08:22,260 --> 00:08:26,300
That's it? You know, it seems like all you do lately is drop the ball.

115
00:08:26,620 --> 00:08:29,760
Don't take that tone with me. Who do you think you are?

116
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:33,980
Alan Lemon, the first black man to fly solo across the Atlantic?

117
00:08:37,020 --> 00:08:39,640
No, look, all I'm saying is you're my agent...

118
00:08:40,040 --> 00:08:42,480
...and you're not getting me any auditions and I'm tired of it.

119
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:43,660
What are you saying?

120
00:08:44,060 --> 00:08:52,060
I'm saying that this isn't working for me anymore, okay, Estelle? You're fired. Goodbye.

121
00:08:52,660 --> 00:08:54,700
Man, tough week for Estelle.

122
00:08:58,340 --> 00:08:59,260
Hi. Hi.

123
00:09:00,440 --> 00:09:02,660
Emma left her stuffed T. Rex at my house.

124
00:09:02,900 --> 00:09:06,900
You know she can't sleep without it. Well, she's asleep now.

125
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:10,100
Stop forcing that thing on her. Okay.

126
00:09:12,300 --> 00:09:14,000
You're not gonna believe what happened to me today!

127
00:09:14,200 --> 00:09:17,200
Ralph Lauren called and gave me my job back!

128
00:09:17,364 --> 00:09:21,960
No! I mean, it was the weirdest thing.

129
00:09:21,980 --> 00:09:24,660
They fire me and then out of nowhere, they just hire me back.

130
00:09:25,040 --> 00:09:27,480
I mean, that place must just be falling apart without me!

131
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:31,520
So you're not going to Paris? No, I'm still going!

132
00:09:35,971 --> 00:09:37,632
What?

133
00:09:37,920 --> 00:09:44,020
Well, when the Louis Vuitton people found out that Ralph Lauren wanted me back, they offered me more money!

134
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:45,940
Ross, isn't that great?

135
00:09:56,160 --> 00:10:00,380
Hi. We're buying the house next door. Could we just take a look around?

136
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:03,360
Sure, I'm showing it to someone else right now, but please, look around.

137
00:10:05,160 --> 00:10:06,780
I feel like we're cheating on our house.

138
00:10:08,120 --> 00:10:12,200
If we cheat, shouldn't it be with a hot, younger house that does stuff that our house won't do?

139
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:17,680
Ours is so much better!

140
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:21,980
This living room is smaller, the dining room looks like a cave.

141
00:10:22,420 --> 00:10:23,500
What a hole!

142
00:10:24,080 --> 00:10:26,240
So, what do you think? Love it!

143
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:31,780
We already have one offer. I think the lady upstairs will make another one.

144
00:10:32,200 --> 00:10:33,820
They could be our neighbors. What are they like?

145
00:10:34,260 --> 00:10:37,060
The woman upstairs is nice. She and her husband have two kids.

146
00:10:37,540 --> 00:10:42,260
He's on Wall Street, and she's a-- Oh... my... God!

147
00:10:52,720 --> 00:10:53,180
Sure.

148
00:10:59,860 --> 00:11:01,840
Hey, Joey, you wanna come with me to--?

149
00:11:02,740 --> 00:11:03,500
Are you okay?

150
00:11:04,100 --> 00:11:07,420
Yeah, I just feel bad about firing Estelle.

151
00:11:07,460 --> 00:11:09,140
This has gotta be killing her.

152
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:14,600
No, that wouldn't kill her.

153
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:16,860
A coronary embolism might.

154
00:11:18,380 --> 00:11:21,420
I don't know. She's gotta be taking it hard. I was her only client.

155
00:11:21,700 --> 00:11:23,760
Except for this guy who eats paper.

156
00:11:24,480 --> 00:11:26,080
And I'm guessing he eats more money than he makes.

157
00:11:28,200 --> 00:11:31,320
Look, I know she's not a great agent, but she did stick with me for 10 years.

158
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:37,020
I'm gonna call her and hire her again. No, no, no! Don't call her.

159
00:11:37,110 --> 00:11:40,540
You wait for her to call you. Why?

160
00:11:41,420 --> 00:11:46,300
Because patience is the road to understanding.

161
00:11:47,500 --> 00:11:49,920
Which is the key to a happy heart.

162
00:11:53,100 --> 00:11:54,180
You blow me away.

163
00:11:55,940 --> 00:11:58,760
All right, so promise you're gonna wait for her to call you?

164
00:11:58,980 --> 00:11:59,980
I promise. Okay.

165
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:03,300
And that means never having to give a reason.

166
00:12:07,940 --> 00:12:10,300
What a small world!

167
00:12:10,600 --> 00:12:12,540
And yet I never run into Beyoncé.

168
00:12:15,740 --> 00:12:16,960
You two know each other?

169
00:12:17,380 --> 00:12:23,360
We go way back. Before Monica made an honest man out of him, Chandler used to be my little love muffin.

170
00:12:26,180 --> 00:12:28,640
So are you guys thinking of getting this house too?

171
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:32,640
Are we gonna have a bidding war? I better warn you, I'm a toughie.

172
00:12:33,140 --> 00:12:34,580
Don't-- Don't-- Don't--

173
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:37,340
No, actually, we are buying the house next door.

174
00:12:38,282 --> 00:12:39,382
Why?

175
00:12:42,520 --> 00:12:44,020
I don't know why.

176
00:12:44,900 --> 00:12:48,440
Oh, that decides it, then! I was on the fence.

177
00:12:48,700 --> 00:12:50,900
But knowing that you two would be our neighbors?

178
00:12:51,480 --> 00:12:52,880
Now we have to get it!

179
00:12:53,780 --> 00:12:55,740
Ellen, we're gonna talk numbers.

180
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:02,500
This cannot be happening.

181
00:13:03,640 --> 00:13:05,500
The realtor said another couple made an offer.

182
00:13:05,720 --> 00:13:10,480
Maybe the Janices won't get it! Maybe the other couple will.

183
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:14,120
The only way that'll happen is if the other couple are the Hitlers!

184
00:13:17,960 --> 00:13:20,720
Okay, okay! All right, what if we get both houses?

185
00:13:20,980 --> 00:13:24,360
We can turn this one into a guesthouse! That's a great idea!

186
00:13:24,740 --> 00:13:26,740
By the way, I don't mean to sound distasteful but...

187
00:13:26,740 --> 00:13:28,420
...when did you start crapping money?

188
00:13:31,120 --> 00:13:32,640
You come up with an idea. All right.

189
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:35,580
We've still got three hours till escrow closes on our house.

190
00:13:36,220 --> 00:13:38,660
We can still get out. But we love our house.

191
00:13:38,680 --> 00:13:40,440
Will we love it so much with her next door?

192
00:13:40,700 --> 00:13:42,620
And she's gonna be louder out here too!

193
00:13:42,640 --> 00:13:45,860
Just the crickets and, "Oh... my... God!"

194
00:13:47,600 --> 00:13:51,160
Okay. But if we don't get this house, she'll still show up wherever we go.

195
00:13:51,740 --> 00:13:54,300
At least if she's here, it eliminates the element of surprise.

196
00:13:55,080 --> 00:13:56,980
Never again will you have to hear the three words...

197
00:13:57,020 --> 00:13:59,340
...that make your balls jump back up inside your body!

198
00:14:06,330 --> 00:14:09,220
Well, we have to do something. We can't have her living next door.

199
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:14,960
Oh, that does it too.

200
00:14:23,100 --> 00:14:24,000
Who is it?

201
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:25,820
It's me. Ron.

202
00:14:29,060 --> 00:14:30,740
Look, I know Rachel turned you down...

203
00:14:30,740 --> 00:14:34,520
...but I think there's a way you might be able to get her to come back.

204
00:14:34,600 --> 00:14:39,900
This may surprise you, but rehiring fired employees is not my main job.

205
00:14:41,600 --> 00:14:42,660
Just hear me out.

206
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:46,300
How would you feel about offering her a raise?

207
00:14:52,090 --> 00:14:52,860
Not good, Ron.

208
00:14:54,780 --> 00:14:56,460
Perhaps I can persuade you.

209
00:14:57,140 --> 00:14:59,100
What if you could give your son...

210
00:14:59,969 --> 00:15:05,771
...this genuine pterodactyl egg replica.

211
00:15:12,600 --> 00:15:14,980
Wow! That's pretty cool.

212
00:15:16,220 --> 00:15:18,500
So? Do we have a deal?

213
00:15:19,580 --> 00:15:22,560
Okay, you got it. Yes! Yes!

214
00:15:22,920 --> 00:15:24,840
This is gonna make me very popular.

215
00:15:26,080 --> 00:15:28,240
Believe me, the ladies, they love it.

216
00:15:32,740 --> 00:15:34,480
I meant with my son.

217
00:15:35,760 --> 00:15:37,220
Good, because the ladies, not so much.

218
00:15:46,134 --> 00:15:51,436
Joey, this is Al Zebooker, the guy who eats paper.

219
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,020
I'm sure you've heard that Estelle passed away.

220
00:15:55,060 --> 00:15:57,440
I wanted to let you know there's a memorial for her...

221
00:15:57,560 --> 00:16:00,060
...at the Westside Chapel tomorrow at 10.

222
00:16:01,380 --> 00:16:02,580
Hope you can make it.

223
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:05,180
Oh, my God.

224
00:16:14,434 --> 00:16:18,029
Hello? Joey, it's Estelle.

225
00:16:23,420 --> 00:16:24,100
Estelle?

226
00:16:25,120 --> 00:16:30,440
Yeah, I wanted to call and tell you that there's no hard feelings for firing me.

227
00:16:34,470 --> 00:16:39,237
Okay. I just-- I can't believe you're calling me.

228
00:16:39,480 --> 00:16:41,440
I didn't think I should just drop by.

229
00:16:41,440 --> 00:16:43,100
No, no! Don't drop by! Don't drop by!

230
00:16:46,160 --> 00:16:49,480
Anyway, you did what you had to do. I'm okay.

231
00:16:50,440 --> 00:16:51,660
Can I ask you something?

232
00:16:54,080 --> 00:16:54,980
What's it like there?

233
00:16:56,780 --> 00:16:57,820
It's all right.

234
00:16:59,560 --> 00:17:01,140
Look, kiddo, I gotta go.

235
00:17:01,620 --> 00:17:04,440
Good luck with the career. You're gonna be huge!

236
00:17:04,680 --> 00:17:07,440
Thanks for everything, Estelle. Bye.

237
00:17:10,880 --> 00:17:12,460
"Out of area." Boy, I'll say!

238
00:17:18,900 --> 00:17:21,180
Well, I just talked to Sid.

239
00:17:21,340 --> 00:17:24,180
We are definitely putting in an offer on the house.

240
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:26,240
And I'll bet we get it!

241
00:17:26,400 --> 00:17:28,020
The Hitlers will be so disappointed.

242
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:33,720
All right, I gotta run. Tell Monica I say goodbye.

243
00:17:34,800 --> 00:17:36,580
I'll see you later, neighbor.

244
00:17:38,300 --> 00:17:39,892
Wait!

245
00:17:40,280 --> 00:17:44,860
I just want you to know that I'm so happy you're going to be here.

246
00:17:45,020 --> 00:17:46,660
Oh, me too!

247
00:17:47,217 --> 00:17:48,946
Because...

248
00:17:48,980 --> 00:17:50,080
...that way...

249
00:17:51,360 --> 00:17:53,440
...we can pick up where we left off.

250
00:17:58,980 --> 00:18:00,780
I never stopped loving you.

251
00:18:01,780 --> 00:18:03,580
Oh... my-- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

252
00:18:06,780 --> 00:18:08,140
I want you.

253
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:10,460
I need you.

254
00:18:11,140 --> 00:18:15,000
I must have you, Janice Litman-Goralnik neé Hosenstein.

255
00:18:20,620 --> 00:18:22,540
Chandler, what are you talking about?

256
00:18:23,540 --> 00:18:26,640
Now that you live next door, we can be together every day.

257
00:18:26,780 --> 00:18:28,580
Sid and Monica never have to know a thing.

258
00:18:29,200 --> 00:18:30,620
I don't know what to say.

259
00:18:32,094 --> 00:18:38,556
I mean, you know, obviously we have this heat between us.

260
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:44,600
Obviously.

261
00:18:46,560 --> 00:18:47,940
But I love my husband.

262
00:18:48,860 --> 00:18:50,800
And I know you love your wife.

263
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,380
I don't think we should get this house now.

264
00:18:54,540 --> 00:18:56,520
Don't say that. Don't dangle the dream and take it away.

265
00:18:59,620 --> 00:19:02,380
Chandler, one of us has got to be strong.

266
00:19:03,060 --> 00:19:04,020
I understand.

267
00:19:07,312 --> 00:19:08,973
Although....

268
00:19:09,960 --> 00:19:11,800
Maybe just one last moment of weakness.

269
00:19:25,140 --> 00:19:26,240
Goodbye, Chandler Bing.

270
00:19:32,220 --> 00:19:33,860
They're never coming down now!

271
00:19:38,180 --> 00:19:39,720
Hi. Hi!

272
00:19:39,720 --> 00:19:43,780
You are never going to believe what happened to me today.

273
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:44,420
What?

274
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:48,300
Ralph Lauren called, again, and they offered me more money.

275
00:19:48,467 --> 00:19:53,534
They did? Yes! It was the weirdest thing.

276
00:19:53,780 --> 00:19:57,120
Zelner called me and said, "We will do anything we can to get you back."

277
00:19:57,220 --> 00:19:59,200
And that I should thank some "Ron."

278
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:02,120
I don't even know what department that guy's in!

279
00:20:04,140 --> 00:20:06,860
So, what are you gonna do? Well, I took it!

280
00:20:07,100 --> 00:20:09,560
That's great! So you're staying in New York!

281
00:20:14,847 --> 00:20:19,682
You're excited, right? You know-- Look, the money's great.

282
00:20:19,900 --> 00:20:22,300
It's certainly the easier choice. Right.

283
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:27,160
But, you know, I mean, was I looking forward to going to Paris? Sure.

284
00:20:28,440 --> 00:20:32,100
Was I excited about working in the fashion capital of the world?

285
00:20:32,280 --> 00:20:33,240
Oh, absolutely.

286
00:20:38,220 --> 00:20:40,720
Yeah, but you know, this is....

287
00:20:40,920 --> 00:20:48,360
It's fine. I'm fine going back to a job where I've pretty much gotten everything out of it that I possibly can.

288
00:20:53,300 --> 00:20:54,140
I had no idea--

289
00:20:56,500 --> 00:21:00,220
I had no idea you were so excited about Paris.

290
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:04,640
I mean, you said you were scared.

291
00:21:05,820 --> 00:21:07,440
I mean, it was "good" scared, though, you know?

292
00:21:07,460 --> 00:21:10,300
I mean, like "when I moved to New York" scared.

293
00:21:11,380 --> 00:21:14,820
Or "when I found out I was gonna have Emma" scared.

294
00:21:18,360 --> 00:21:21,180
But this is fine. This is gonna be good.

295
00:21:27,860 --> 00:21:29,860
You should go.

296
00:21:31,640 --> 00:21:33,198
What?

297
00:21:34,900 --> 00:21:36,840
It's what you want. You should go.

298
00:21:38,700 --> 00:21:39,640
You really think so?

299
00:21:41,560 --> 00:21:42,440
I really do.

300
00:21:44,480 --> 00:21:46,920
But I already told Zelner that I would come back.

301
00:21:47,720 --> 00:21:49,180
I'm sure he'll understand.

302
00:21:55,613 --> 00:22:00,915
All right. All right! I'm gonna do it! I'm gonna go to Paris!

303
00:22:02,240 --> 00:22:03,600
Yeah. Yeah.

304
00:22:04,260 --> 00:22:05,220
I'm going to Paris!

305
00:22:06,680 --> 00:22:08,960
Thank you, Ross. Yeah, yeah.

306
00:22:12,520 --> 00:22:13,860
Oh, I'm so happy.

307
00:22:16,280 --> 00:22:17,580
Then I'm happy too.

308
00:22:29,200 --> 00:22:30,620
Thank you all for coming.

309
00:22:31,840 --> 00:22:36,620
We're here today to pay our respects to a wonderful agent...and a beautiful woman.

310
00:22:39,280 --> 00:22:39,840
Inside.

311
00:22:43,500 --> 00:22:47,280
As Estelle's only two clients, we would like to say a few words.

312
00:22:50,120 --> 00:22:51,480
Dude, where's my speech?

313
00:22:57,400 --> 00:22:59,980
That is entertaining. Al Zebooker, everybody!

